{UTF-8}
Actual Biomass Growth Fraction  = 
        Baseline Biomass Growth Fraction 
             * Kaibab Adequacy for Forage 
	~	1/Year
	~	Regrowth fraction after scaling for how close forage is to the forest's \
		carrying capacity.
	|

Actual Consumption per Deer  = 
        Desired Consumption per Deer 
             * Effect of Forage Availability on Consumption 
	~	Metric Tons / (Deer * Year)
	~	Forage actually eaten per deer per year, reduced below the desired amount \
		when forage is scarce.
	|

Actual Fractional Net Growth Rate  = 
        Baseline Fractional Deer Growth Rate 
             * Kaibab Adequacy to Support the Deer Population 
	~	1/Year
	~	Fractional net growth rate after scaling for how adequate the Kaibab is to \
		support further growth.
	|

Auxilliary Time to Deer  = 1
	~	Deer/Year
	~	Auxilliary parameter to convert Year into Deer for the time series lookup.
	|

Baseline Annual Kills per Predator  = :NA:
	~	Deer / (Predators * Year) [20,60,0.1]
	~	The average number of deer killed per predator in a given year.
	|

Baseline Biomass Growth Fraction  = :NA:
	~	1/Year [0.05,0.25,0.01]
	~	Fractional net regrowth rate of forage when the forest is far below its \
		own carrying capacity.
	|

Baseline Fractional Deer Growth Rate  = :NA:
	~	1/Year [0.5,1.5,0.01]
	~	Fractional net growth rate of the deer herd when forage is abundant.
	|

Baseline Fractional Predator Growth  = :NA:
	~	1/Year [0.5,1.5,0.01]
	~	Fraction by which the predator population grows each year when deer are \
		abundant.
	|

Deer Carrying Capacity in Years  = :NA:
	~	Year [1,7,0.1]
	~	Number of years of forage needed to sustain the deer population.
	|

Deer Density  = 
        Deer Population 
             / Kaibab Area in Thousands of Acres 
             / Normal Deer Density per Acre 
	~	dmnl
	~	The ratio of deer to area, all of which is divided by a normal. Results in \
		a scale where one is normal, anything above 1 indicates too many deer, and \
		anything below 1 indicates too few deer compared to what is normal deer \
		per acres.
	|

Deer Net Growth Rate  = 
        Deer Population 
             * Actual Fractional Net Growth Rate 
	~	Deer/Year
	~	Biflow. Net deer added or removed per year through births and natural \
		deaths, before predation.
	|

Deer Population  = 
        INTEG( Deer Net Growth Rate 
                  - Deer Predation , 
             Initial Deer Population ) 
	~	Deer
	~	The number/stock of mule deer on the Kaibab Plateau. Increased by Deer Net \
		Growth, decreased by Deer Predation.
	|

Deer Predation  = 
        Baseline Annual Kills per Predator 
             * Predator Population 
             * Effect of Deer Density on Predator Hunting Efficiency 
	~	Deer/Year
	~	Outflow from the Deer Population. Number of deer killed by predators each \
		year.
	|

Desired Consumption per Deer  = :NA:
	~	Metric Tons / (Deer * Year) [0.4,0.8,0.01]
	~	The amount of forage consumed by a single deer in a year, when abundant \
		forage is available.
	|

Effect of Deer Density on Predator Births  = 
        Lookup Effect of Deer Density on Predator Births ( Deer Density ) 
	~	dmnl
	~	Multiplier on the Net Predator Growth Rate. -1.0 at deer density 0, 0 at \
		density 1 (births equal deaths), 1.5 at density 2 or more.
	|

Effect of Deer Density on Predator Hunting Efficiency  = 
        Lookup Effect of Deer Density on Predator Hunting Efficiency ( Deer Density ) 
	~	dmnl
	~	Multiplier on Deer Predation. 0 when no deer are present, 1 at normal \
		density, rising to 1.5 when deer are abundant; the slope flattens at both \
		ends.
	|

Effect of Forage Availability on Consumption  = 
        Lookup Effect of Forage Availability on Consumption ( Ratio of Actual to Required Biomass\
		 ) 
	~	dmnl
	~	This nonlinear or graphical function captures how Actual Consumption per \
		Deer will change given the Ratio of Actual to Required Biomass. Deer will \
		continue to eat a normal amount of forage until only about 30 percent of \
		what is needed is available. Deer will then start to eat less as foraging \
		becomes more difficult.
	|

Forage Biomass  = 
        INTEG( Net Annual Forage Growth 
                  - Forage Consumed per Deer , 
             Initial Forage Biomass ) 
	~	Metric Tons
	~	Forage available for deer to eat
	|

Forage Consumed per Deer  = 
        Deer Population 
             * Actual Consumption per Deer 
	~	Metric Tons/Year
	~	Total forage eaten by the whole herd each year. Despite the name this is a \
		total, not a per-head rate.
	|

Fraction Predators Killed per Year  = :NA:
	~	1/Year [0,0.5,0.01]
	~	Fraction of predators killed each year.
	|

Historical Deer BOT 
	~	Deer
	~	A reference mode showing the actual deer population estimates from 1900 \
		through 1950. This is defined by the graphical function shown in the \
		source. You can display this BOTG with the Deer Population to help tune \
		the model parameters to match the historical record.
	~	:SUPPLEMENTARY 
	|

Initial Deer Population  = GET XLS CONSTANTS('config/parameters/initial_stock_parameters.xlsx'\
		, 
                'Kaibab Variables', 
                'C7')
	~	Deer
	~	Exogenous variable used to set (and change) the initial size of the Deer \
		Population.
	|

Initial Forage Biomass  = GET XLS CONSTANTS('config/parameters/initial_stock_parameters.xlsx'\
		, 
                'Kaibab Variables', 
                'C9')
	~	Metric Tons
	~		|

Initial Predator Population  = GET XLS CONSTANTS('config/parameters/initial_stock_parameters.xlsx'\
		, 
                'Kaibab Variables', 
                'C11')
	~	Predators [20,130,1]
	~	Used to set the initial size of the Predator Population. Notice that this \
		is different from the original predator-prey model you built.
	|

Kaibab Adequacy for Forage  = 
        1
             - Ratio of Forage to Forage Carrying Capacity 
	~	dmnl
	~	Approaches 1 when forage is far below its carrying capacity and 0 as it \
		reaches it.
	|

Kaibab Adequacy to Support the Deer Population  = 
        1
             - Ratio of the Deer Population to Carrying Capacity 
	~	dmnl
	~	Approaches 1 when the herd is far below carrying capacity and 0 as it \
		reaches carrying capacity.
	|

Kaibab Area in Thousands of Acres  = :NA:
	~	Thousands of Acres
	~	Land area of the Kaibab Plateau. This is approximately 800,000 acres. We \
		are measuring this in thousands of acres, which is why we use 800 when \
		setting the value.
	|

Kaibab Deer Carrying Capacity  = 
        Forage Biomass 
             / ( Desired Consumption per Deer 
                  * Deer Carrying Capacity in Years ) 
	~	Deer
	~	Number of deer the current forage stock can sustain for the required \
		number of years. Endogenous: it erodes as forage is consumed.
	|

Kaibab Forage Carrying Capacity  = :NA:
	~	Metric Tons [200000,600000,1000]
	~	Amount of forage biomass that the Kaibab can support on an ongoing basis.
	|

Lookup Effect of Deer Density on Predator Births  ( 
            [(0,0)-(10,10)],(0,0),(1,0) )
	~	dmnl
	~	Lookup for: Multiplier on the Net Predator Growth Rate. -1.0 at deer \
		density 0, 0 at density 1 (births equal deaths), 1.5 at density 2 or more.
	|

Lookup Effect of Deer Density on Predator Hunting Efficiency  ( 
            [(0,0)-(10,10)],(0,0),(1,0) )
	~	dmnl
	~	Lookup for: Multiplier on Deer Predation. 0 when no deer are present, 1 at \
		normal density, rising to 1.5 when deer are abundant; the slope flattens \
		at both ends.
	|

Lookup Effect of Forage Availability on Consumption  ( 
            [(0,0)-(1,1)],(0,0),(1,0) )
	~	dmnl
	~	Vensim Lookup for: This nonlinear or graphical function captures how \
		Actual Consumption per Deer will change given the Ratio of Actual to \
		Required Biomass. Deer will continue to eat a normal amount of forage \
		until only about 30 percent of what is needed is available. Deer will then \
		start to eat less as foraging becomes more difficult.
	|

Lookup Historical Deer BOT  ( 
            [(1900,0)-(1950,90000)],(1900,4000),(1902.5,4000),(1905,4000),(1907.5,5000)\
		,(1910,9000),(1912.5,15000),(1915,25000),(1917.5,35000),(1920,65000),(1922.5,90000)\
		,(1925,60000),(1927.5,37000),(1930,25000),(1932.5,20000),(1935,18000),(1937.5,12000)\
		,(1940,10000),(1942.5,7000),(1945,5000),(1947.5,3000),(1950,2000) )
	~	Deer
	~	Lookup for: A reference mode showing the actual deer population estimates \
		from 1900 through 1950. This is defined by the graphical function shown in \
		the source. You can display this BOTG with the Deer Population to help \
		tune the model parameters to match the historical record.
	|

Net Annual Forage Growth  = 
        Forage Biomass 
             * Actual Biomass Growth Fraction 
	~	Metric Tons / Year
	~	Net change in the forage biomass on the Kaibab via natural growth and \
		decay, apart from consumption by animals.
	|

Net Predator Growth Rate  = 
        Predator Population 
             * Baseline Fractional Predator Growth 
             * Effect of Deer Density on Predator Births 
	~	Predators / Year
	~	Biflow. Net predators added or removed per year. Negative when deer \
		density falls below normal, i.e., predators starve.
	|

Normal Deer Density per Acre  = :NA:
	~	Deer/Thousands of Acres
	~	Number of deer per 1000 acres on the Kaibab
	|

Predator Population  = 
        INTEG( Net Predator Growth Rate 
                  - Predators Hunted , 
             Initial Predator Population ) 
	~	Predators
	~	Number of predators on the Kaibab Plateau
	|

Predators Hunted  = 
        STEP ( Predator Population 
                  * Fraction Predators Killed per Year , 
             Program Start Year ) 
	~	Predators/Year
	~	Number of predators removed each year through hunting.
	|

Program Start Year  = :NA:
	~	Year [1905,1920,1]
	~	The year the predator hunting policy is implemented. From that year on, a \
		fraction of the remaining predators will be hunted and killed each year.
	|

Ratio of Actual to Required Biomass  = 
        ZIDZ ( Forage Biomass , 
             Required Forage Biomass ) 
	~	dmnl
	~	Measures how much forage is available, relative to what is needed to \
		sustain the current deer population. Values below 1 indicate a shortage of \
		forage biomass.
	|

Ratio of Forage to Forage Carrying Capacity  = 
        Forage Biomass 
             / Kaibab Forage Carrying Capacity 
	~	dmnl
	~	How close the forage stock is to the maximum the Kaibab forest can carry.
	|

Ratio of the Deer Population to Carrying Capacity  = 
        ZIDZ ( Deer Population , 
             Kaibab Deer Carrying Capacity ) 
	~	dmnl
	~	How close the herd is to what the forage can support.
	|

Required Forage Biomass  = 
        Deer Population 
             * Desired Consumption per Deer 
             * Deer Carrying Capacity in Years 
	~	Metric Tons
	~	Forage biomass needed to sustain the existing deer population for the \
		number of years indicated by the value of the Deer Carrying Capacity in \
		Years.
	|

********************************************************
	.Control
********************************************************~
		Simulation Control Parameters
	|

FINAL TIME  = 1950
	~	Year
	~	The final time for the simulation.
	|

INITIAL TIME  = 1900
	~	Year
	~	The initial time for the simulation.
	|

SAVEPER  = 
        TIME STEP 
	~	Year [0,?]
	~	The frequency with which output is stored.
	|

TIME STEP  = 0.05
	~	Year [0,?]
	~	The time step for the simulation.
	|

